---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Evidence from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Evidence.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { emph } from "../lib/text.js";
import dgs from "../data/dgs-audit.json";
import traps from "../data/forename-traps.json";
import zeros from "../data/false-zeros.json";
import certs from "../data/1852-aguada-union-certificates.json";
---
<Base title="Method"
      eyebrow="How this archive decides what it will say"
      dek="The same method as the Falco and Defranceski archives. Every claim is tiered, and the tier is shown. An archive that cannot say what it does not know is not an archive.">

  <section class="blk" id="ladder">
    <h2>The five words</h2>
    <p class="prose">All seven of these family archives grade a claim with the same five words, so a
    level means the same thing wherever you meet it. The word is always printed next to the colour,
    because a reader should never have to know the palette to know the confidence.</p>
    <dl class="ladder">
      <dt><Evidence level="documented" /></dt>
      <dd>A record was read, and it says this. The source is named.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="probable" /></dt>
      <dd>The evidence points one way and nothing found so far points the other. Not proven.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="inferred" /></dt>
      <dd>Reasoned from what is known — a gap of the right size, a name in the right place — rather
      than witnessed in a record.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="family" /></dt>
      <dd>Told, remembered, or written on a family chart. Kept, because discarding it would lose the
      lead; labelled, because it is not evidence.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="disputed" /></dt>
      <dd>Two sources disagree and neither has been ruled out. Both are shown.</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="notclaimed">
      <span class="lab">Not claimed</span>
      <p class="prose">Some pages carry this instead. It states the thing a reader is likely to
      assume — the connection that would be tidy, the identification that almost works — and says
      plainly that this archive does not assert it. An archive is judged as much by what it refuses
      to say as by what it says.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>The tiers</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="chip doc">Documented</span></td>
          <td>A named source with a reference, and where possible the scan itself.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip">Inferred</span></td>
          <td>A reasoned conclusion from documented facts, with the reasoning written out so that it can be
            overturned by anyone who finds it wanting.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip">Superseded</span></td>
          <td>Asserted in the family record, and now displaced by a document that says otherwise. The old
            claim is kept visible, not deleted.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip inf">Disputed</span></td>
          <td>Asserted in the family record but unsupported, or contradicted, by what can be seen.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip lore">Family lore</span></td>
          <td>Told, remembered, not corroborated. Kept because it is precious; labelled because pretending
            otherwise is how family myths become family history.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>This archive starts at the bottom of the ladder</h2>
  <p>
    Nearly everything specific to this family is presently <strong>family lore</strong>. The Argentine
    origin, Rosario, the consulship, the horses, the ostriches — all of it is testimony, and it is presented
    as testimony. What is <strong>documented</strong> so far is largely <em>context</em>: the rarity and
    distribution of the surname, the town of Llerena, the Argentine Stud Book, the Oudtshoorn feather boom.
  </p>
  <p>
    Context is not evidence about a family. It is the backdrop against which that family has to be placed,
    and it is worth having early because it tells you which claims are likely, which are hard, and where to
    look. It is never allowed to stand in for a record.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Rarity is evidence, and it has limits</h2>
  <p>
    With 2,124 bearers worldwide, <em>Lerena</em> is rare enough that clustering carries real information —
    the 61 in South Africa are very unlikely to be unrelated strangers. The archive will therefore let
    distribution <strong>propose</strong> connections.
  </p>
  <p>
    It will not let distribution <strong>prove</strong> them. A proposal built on rarity is written down as
    inferred, with its reasoning exposed, and it stays inferred until a register says otherwise.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Living people</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Living people are omitted from the build entirely</strong> — not hidden, not gated, not present
    in the output. Because this line reaches the present within three generations, that removes a
    substantial part of the story from public view, including most of the South African and all of the
    Australian chapter. The material is kept in the private working files; it is simply not published.
  </p>
  <p>
    Public figures are treated differently, and only as public figures: a jockey's race record or a
    breeder's stud book entry is published information and may be cited as such. No claim of relationship to
    a living person is made from a shared surname.
  </p>
  <p>
    A removal request is honoured within days, without argument and without requiring a reason.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2>A null is not a finding until a control says so</h2>
  <p class="dek">Most of what this archive has established is negative &mdash; that Pablo Armando is
    not in the Rosario baptisms, that Juan Carlos Lerena is in no record anywhere. Negative findings
    are only as good as the search that produced them, and a search can fail in a way that looks
    exactly like an answer.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>On 14 September 2026 this archive nearly recorded three false nulls in ten minutes.</strong>
      FamilySearch&rsquo;s results page renders the string
      <em>&ldquo;Historical Record Search Results (0)&rdquo;</em> <strong>while the query is still
      running</strong> &mdash; a complete, composed, confident page that cannot be told apart from a
      genuinely empty result.</p>
    <p><strong>It was caught only by running a control.</strong> The same query shape was aimed at
      <em>Sancho Lerena</em>, a man for whom five records had been pulled twenty minutes earlier. It
      returned zero too. Since <em>that</em> answer was known to be false, the fault had to be in the
      query rather than in the name. Waiting longer returned the five records &mdash; and then 210, and
      then 822.</p>
    <p>This is the same class of failure as the zero-length response body already logged against this
      source: <strong>the service failing in a way that resembles success</strong>. It is worse than an
      outage, because an outage announces itself.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>So the rule: a null is not written down until a control query, in the same
      form, in the same session, returns a result already known to exist.</strong> The archive learned
      this once before and more expensively, over the 1895 census, where it read an enumerator&rsquo;s
      abbreviation as a husband&rsquo;s surname with no control case and had to retract within the hour.
      The second time the lesson arrived it cost nothing.</p>
    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:18px">15 September 2026 &mdash; the rule decided four questions in
      one day, and only one of them the way it was expected to</p>
    <p><strong>It saved a day of work once.</strong> This archive had planned to read three hundred
      images of a regiment&rsquo;s chaplaincy book by hand, looking for Sancho Llerena. Before starting,
      one control &mdash; a search for the chaplain&rsquo;s own name, <em>Berroeta</em> &mdash; showed the
      book <em>was</em> in the full-text index. His absence from it was therefore already evidence, and
      the three hundred images were never read.</p>
    <p><strong>It voided a null that looked clean.</strong> Searching Spanish records for
      <em>Félix Llerena and Rosalía Aguilar</em> returned zero. The control &mdash; asking what Andalusian
      collections exist at all &mdash; showed the full-text corpus for Andalusia holds
      <strong>nobility and notarial records, not parish registers</strong>. The zero meant
      <em>not findable by this instrument</em>, not <em>not there</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>It voided two more, in the same hour.</strong> Two exact-surname searches returned zero
      <em>with</em> the &ldquo;No Results Found&rdquo; line present &mdash; normally this archive&rsquo;s
      proof that a search ran. The control showed the same query shape <strong>could not retrieve a
      record known to be in the collection</strong>, because the indexed surname was a compound
      (<em>Lerena y Armenta</em>) that exact matching will not reach. <strong>A null from a query that
      cannot find what is there is not a null.</strong></p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>And once it confirmed a negative worth keeping.</strong> Sancho&rsquo;s burial
      is in no index. The control &mdash; a common surname, same city, same decade &mdash; returned church
      burials from <em>his own family&rsquo;s parish</em> dated 1832, 1833 and 1838. So the records exist,
      are indexed, and do not contain him. <strong>That is the only one of the four that survived, and it
      is the only one this archive would publish.</strong></p>
    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:18px">21 September 2026 &mdash; the control passed, and the null
      was false anyway</p>
    <p>The rule above says a null is not written down until a control in the same form and the same
      session returns a known result. On 21 September that rule was satisfied and the answer was
      still a lie.</p>
    <p>A parent-pair query on <strong>Avelino Lerena and Celedonia Villademoros</strong> returned a
      clean zero. The control &mdash; the identical query shape on <strong>Alberto Lerena and María
      Inés Larriera</strong>, minutes earlier &mdash; had returned sixteen records. Deleting the
      mother&rsquo;s <em>surname</em> and keeping only &ldquo;Celedonia&rdquo; turned the zero into
      <strong>seven</strong>, two of which are christenings already published on this site.</p>
    <p><strong>So a control licenses the <em>shape</em> of a query and says nothing about the
      <em>value</em> you put in it.</strong> Larriera has one spelling in this register. Villademoros
      has four &mdash; Villademoros, Villardemoros, Villademoro, and the compound the children carry.
      The control could not fail, because it was asked about the one surname that could not go wrong.
      What caught the zero was not method at all: it was that the archive already held two of the
      records it was hiding.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>Which sharpens the older rule rather than replacing it.</strong> A nil from
      a derived index is, in the first instance, <strong>a fact about the index</strong>. It becomes a
      fact about the family only when the index&rsquo;s coverage of the relevant registers has been
      established &mdash; and coverage is not one thing. It can fail at the collection, as it did for
      Andalusian parish registers; at the query shape, as it did for the compound
      <em>Lerena y Armenta</em>; or, as here, <strong>at a single field the source failed to
      parse</strong>. Three different failures, one identical-looking zero.</p>
    <p class="cap">This archive publishes negatives as evidence, because most of what it has
      established is negative. That is exactly why the bar has to keep rising.
      <strong>A published null needs a date, a control, and a statement of what the instrument could
      have reached.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-false-zeros">Every disguise a false zero has worn</h2>
  <p class="dek">The rule above was not arrived at once. It was arrived at twelve times, and this is
    the catalogue &mdash; because a search that fails in a way that looks like an answer is the most
    dangerous thing this archive meets, and the next one will not look like any of these either.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Nine of these were written in working logs where no reader could see them</strong>
      &mdash; found on 20 September 2026 by asking which findings in this archive&rsquo;s 136 working
      files reach no page at all. The two dated that day were caught by the habit rather than by luck,
      which is the only argument for keeping a habit.</p>
    <p class="cap">On 15 September the working log recorded that the same trap had been seen
      <strong>four times in a month, in four different disguises</strong>. It is now twelve.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="width:14rem">The disguise</th><th style="width:16rem">What it looked like</th>
        <th style="width:16rem">What it actually was</th><th>How it was caught</th>
        <th class="m" style="width:6rem">When</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {zeros.map((r) => {
          const c = Object.values(r);
          return (
            <tr>
              <th set:html={emph(c[0])} />
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[1])} />
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[2])} />
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[3])} />
              <td class="m">{c[4]}</td>
            </tr>
          );
        })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>What they have in common is not the mechanism.</strong>
    A quota, a logout, a throttle, a block, a mislabelled reel and a counter that ignores its own
    parameters have nothing to do with one another. What they share is that
    <strong>each produced a page a careful person would have believed</strong>, and that in every single
    case the thing that caught it was a control query whose answer was already known.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--terra);margin:24px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">And the mirror &mdash; a false <em>positive</em> that would
      have been believed</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A full-text search for the exact phrase <em>&ldquo;Sancho Llerena&rdquo;</em> returns four pages.
        Page one is this family. <strong>Pages two to four are Spanish nobility proofs, notarial books and
        military records &mdash; and not one of them contains a man of that name.</strong></p>
      <p>One was opened to find out why. A 1599 proof of nobility for the Order of Alc&aacute;ntara reads,
        in transcript: <em>&ldquo;&hellip;es familiar del <strong>SANCHO, OFICIO LLERENA</strong>&rdquo;</em>.
        It is <strong><em>Santo Oficio &hellip; Llerena</em></strong> &mdash; the Inquisition tribunal seated
        at Llerena in Badajoz. <strong>The machine reads <em>Santo Oficio</em> as <em>Sancho, oficio</em>,
        and the phrase search then matches <em>Sancho &hellip; Llerena</em> across the two words.</strong></p>
      <p>The Inquisition sat at Llerena for three centuries, so those words stand next to each other in
        thousands of Spanish documents. <strong>Any search for this family in Spain will drown in
        them</strong> &mdash; and this archive was one step from recording that <em>Sancho</em> is an
        attested forename in Spanish Llerena families. It is not. That false fact would then have been
        used, quietly, to argue against <strong>Francisco</strong>.</p>
      <p class="cap">A false zero is caught by a control. <strong>A false positive is caught only by
        opening the document</strong> &mdash; which is the other half of the same rule, and the more
        expensive half, because a hit that agrees with you asks to be believed.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-zero-for-everything">The afternoon the search returned nothing, and said so politely</h2>
  <p class="dek">It passed every guard this archive had. The only thing that caught it was a control
    whose answer cannot be zero.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>On the afternoon of <strong>21 September 2026</strong>, a query returned
      <em>&ldquo;Historical Record Search Results (0)&rdquo;</em> with <strong>&ldquo;No Results
      Found&rdquo;</strong> printed beneath it.</p>
    <p>That line is the thing this archive has been treating as proof of a <em>real</em> null &mdash;
      the positive assertion of emptiness, as opposed to a heading rendered before the data arrives.
      The count was <strong>stable across two reads</strong> twelve seconds apart. It was still zero
      after a <strong>ninety-second wait</strong>. And a control on the same query shape had
      <strong>passed earlier the same day</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>All of it was wrong.</strong> The search was returning zero for everything.</p>
    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:18px">What it actually was</p>
    <p><strong>One browser context had lost its authentication &mdash; and only one.</strong> The two
      FamilySearch searches handle that completely differently: asked the same question in the same
      minute, the <strong>full-text search redirected to the sign-in page</strong> &mdash; honest,
      unmistakable, one navigation &mdash; while the <strong>record search answered
      &ldquo;0 &mdash; No Results Found&rdquo;</strong>. The DeepZoom image host went on serving
      plates throughout, which is why a whole day of reading images had given no warning.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>So there is a free test, and it costs one navigation:</strong> ask the
      full-text search anything at all. It admits a lost session. The record search hides one behind
      a null that satisfies every other rule on this page.</p>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>And this archive then got the remedy wrong,
      which is the part worth publishing.</strong> It concluded that the <em>account</em> was signed
      out and said so here, and on its work list, and that David would have to sign in again.
      <strong>The account was never signed out.</strong> A sibling archive on the same machine ran
      both searches in those same minutes and got <strong>3,652</strong> and <strong>3,967</strong>,
      with the account name in the navigation bar. Each session&rsquo;s in-app browser is a separate
      browser with its own cookies; one lost its auth and recovered by itself. In a fresh tab the
      Gonz&aacute;lez control returned <strong>479,662</strong> and the query that had returned ten
      that morning returned <strong>ten</strong> again. <strong>Before asking a person to fix
      something, open a second tab.</strong></div>
    <p class="cap">All three nulls held over for re-testing &mdash; Luis with Carolina, Carlos
      Ambrosio with Luisa, Juan Arturo with Isidora &mdash; were re-run against a live control and all
      three are still zero. Nothing was lost but the afternoon.</p>
    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:18px">And the next day, the same guard caught a different fault</p>
    <p>A parent-pair query on <strong>Sancho</strong> with <strong>Mar&iacute;a</strong> in Uruguay
      returned <strong>0</strong> &mdash; read twice, with the Gonz&aacute;lez control returning
      479,660 minutes earlier. This time the service was healthy. <strong>The query shape was
      broken.</strong></p>
    <ul class="prose" style="margin-left:1.1rem">
      <li>father&rsquo;s given name <em>Sancho</em>, alone &mdash; <strong>10,315</strong></li>
      <li>mother&rsquo;s given name <em>Mar&iacute;a</em>, alone &mdash; <strong>1,228,708</strong></li>
      <li>both together &mdash; <strong>0</strong></li>
      <li>both, <em>plus the two surnames</em> &mdash; <strong>6</strong>, every one of them real</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Two fields matching five and seven figures cannot intersect at nothing in a country where
      <em>Mar&iacute;a</em> is the commonest name a mother has. <strong>A parent-pair query with given
      names only returns zero whatever the truth is</strong>, and says nothing about it. At least one
      parent <em>surname</em> must be supplied before a null from this shape means anything.</p>
    <p class="cap">Arithmetic caught it again, and it is the only guard on this page that has never
      failed. Notice what it is doing: not asking whether the answer is plausible, but whether the
      answer is <em>possible</em>.</p>

    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:18px">Three things the other archives on this machine sent back,
      each better than what this one had</p>
    <ul class="prose" style="margin-left:1.1rem">
      <li><strong>A negative must carry proof that the instrument reached the place.</strong> Not
        &ldquo;the search returned nothing&rdquo; but <em>here is the entry before where it would be,
        and here is the entry after, with the numbering continuous across the page break</em>. That is
        a null carrying its own evidence. A control query only <em>borrows</em> evidence from
        something run nearby. <span class="cap">(Luwinski.)</span></li>
      <li><strong>A positive proves the service was answering <em>for that collection</em>.</strong>
        Where access is per-collection rather than all-or-nothing, a hit in an open collection vouches
        for nothing in a restricted one. <span class="cap">(Defranceski.)</span></li>
      <li><strong>A service that is honest in one place and fabricates in another, at the same
        moment, is worse than one that always lies.</strong> A consistent liar is caught once and
        distrusted for ever. This one redirected honestly in one browser and manufactured a null in
        another, in the same minute, on the same machine.
        <span class="cap">(Booyzen.)</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="cap"><strong>And the thing that hid it longest, which is worth more than any of the
      rules:</strong> the plate host went on serving images perfectly all afternoon, in the same
      browser, while the search fabricated. <strong>A day of successful image reads is not evidence
      that the search is healthy.</strong> Different hosts, different authentication, independent
      failure &mdash; and the one you are watching is not the one that is lying.</p>
    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:18px">What proved it</p>
    <p>Two facts, neither of them a matter of judgement:</p>
    <ul class="prose" style="margin-left:1.1rem">
      <li><strong>Surname <em>Gonz&aacute;lez</em>, in Uruguay, returned 0.</strong> Gonz&aacute;lez
        is among the commonest surnames in the Spanish-speaking world; the Uruguayan civil
        registration runs to millions of records. <strong>That answer cannot be zero.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>The same URL that had returned ten records that morning returned 0 that
        afternoon</strong> &mdash; Avelino Lerena with Ambrosia Traibel, the ten acts this archive had
        spent the day reading.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:18px">What it nearly cost</p>
    <p>The zeros looked like a discovery. A man whose baptism this archive has <em>read off the
      plate</em> &mdash; Alberto Octavio Lerena, act 585 of 7 October 1850 &mdash; returned nothing
      when searched by name. The obvious reading was a real and interesting mechanism: that the
      parent-pair query reaches only the <em>keyed</em> index, and cannot see acts that exist in the
      full-text corpus alone.</p>
    <p><strong>That is a good theory, it explains the evidence, and it is false.</strong> It was
      about to be written up as a finding, with consequences for every null in this archive drawn
      from a parent-pair query. It was built entirely on an outage.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>So the rule gains two words.</strong> A control must be <strong>recent</strong>
      &mdash; the service was healthy in the morning and lying by the afternoon &mdash; and its answer
      must be <strong>arithmetically impossible to be zero</strong>. A control that merely
      <em>ought</em> to return something is a control that fails silently alongside the query it was
      meant to guard.</p>
    <p class="cap">One class of zero survives untouched, and it is worth naming: <strong>a zero that
      was turned into a result by deleting a parameter</strong>. Villademoros 0&rarr;7 and
      Hermenegilda 0&rarr;3 both stand, because a positive result proves the service was answering at
      that moment. <strong>Only the zeros that stayed zero are in doubt.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-machine-read">How much of this register has a machine read, and nobody checked</h2>
  <p class="dek">Two failures on one day, from the same cause, pointing in opposite directions.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This archive&rsquo;s standing rule is that an index is not evidence and the image must be
      opened. On <strong>21 September 2026</strong> it found out how often it had kept that rule.</p>
    <p>Sixty-one register rows cited <em>&ldquo;full-text transcript read&rdquo;</em> &mdash; which
      means <strong>a machine read it</strong> &mdash; and named no image. That is more than half the
      rows carrying a source at all. A tool now ranks the unopened plates by how many people depend on
      each one, because <strong>one image can settle seven rows at once</strong>.</p>
    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:18px">What the machine got wrong</p>
    <p>The 1892 marriage act of Alfredo Justiniano Lerena Traibel gave his bride as
      <strong>Hermenegilda Carolina Gavarro</strong>, daughter of <strong>Ger&oacute;nimo
      Gavarro</strong> and <strong>Carolina Ju&aacute;rez</strong>. The plate says
      <em>&ldquo;hija leg&iacute;tima de Don <strong>Ger&oacute;nimo Gavazzo</strong> y Do&ntilde;a
      <strong>Carolina Su&aacute;rez</strong>&rdquo;</em>. Three surnames, wrong for a week, and
      confirmed wrong by a second image forty-eight years later. <strong>Gavazzo is a known
      Montevideo surname and Gavarro is not</strong>, which is the kind of tell that should have
      prompted the check.</p>
    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:18px">What the machine got wrong in the other direction</p>
    <p>The top-ranked unopened plate was the one carrying the archive&rsquo;s most consequential
      proper noun: the 1831 baptism at the Cord&oacute;n where <strong>the President of Uruguay
      stood godfather</strong> to Sancho&rsquo;s grandson. Seven register rows rest on it. It was
      opened &mdash; film 007713388, image 00726 &mdash; and the act reads:</p>
    <p style="margin-left:1.2rem"><em>&ldquo;padrinos Dn. <strong>Frutuso Ribera, Presidente de la
      Rep&uacute;blica</strong> y su esposa Da. <strong>Bernardina Fragoso</strong>&rdquo;</em></p>
    <p><strong>Frutuso Ribera, with a B.</strong> The transcript had printed <em>Fructuoso
      Rivera</em> &mdash; the spelling in every history book, and not what the page says.
      <strong>The finding holds and is stronger than it was</strong>, because the act states the
      man&rsquo;s <em>office</em> and names his <em>wife</em>: in December 1831 the President of the
      Republic was Fructuoso Rivera and his wife was Bernardina Fragoso. Two independent facts on one
      line, either of which identifies him alone. The same page spells the grandfather
      <strong>Llerena</strong>, with the double L.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>Both failures are one failure.</strong> A machine transcript is reliable
      about dates, ages, relationships and formulae, and unreliable about <em>proper nouns</em> &mdash;
      it invents a plausible name where it cannot read one, and it writes the famous spelling over the
      page&rsquo;s own where it can. <strong>So the rule is not &ldquo;distrust transcripts&rdquo;. It
      is: a transcript is a finding-aid for everything except the names, and the names are usually why
      you went.</strong></p>
    <p class="lab" style="margin-top:18px">The sweep is finished &mdash; sixty-one to nothing</p>
    <p><strong>Every one of those rows has now been read off a plate.</strong> Twenty images, in one
      day. The tool reports <strong>0 of 153</strong>, and what the sweep actually cost is the
      argument for having run it:</p>
    <ul class="prose" style="margin-left:1.1rem">
      <li><strong>Four claims about this family&rsquo;s spelling were the machine&rsquo;s, not the
        scribe&rsquo;s</strong> &mdash; <em>Abelino Serena</em> is LERENA; <em>Sancho Lorena</em> is
        LERENA; the <em>LORENA</em> of an 1889 act is its margin, not its sentence; and
        <em>Fructuoso Rivera</em> is the history books&rsquo; spelling written over the page&rsquo;s
        own <strong>Frutuso Ribera</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Five surnames were simply wrong.</strong> Gavarro is <strong>Gavazzo</strong>,
        Ju&aacute;rez is <strong>Su&aacute;rez</strong>, Florencia is <strong>Florinda</strong>,
        Seguera is <strong>Segueira</strong>, and &ldquo;Del Suando&rdquo; is
        <strong>Zalsamendi</strong> &mdash; a Basque name no search for the other could have reached.</li>
      <li><strong>One baptism was in the wrong parish</strong> and one citation pointed at the wrong
        film.</li>
      <li><strong>A birthplace was wrong in a way that changed the family&rsquo;s shape:</strong>
        Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez is <em>&ldquo;natural de Asturias&rdquo;</em>, not of Montevideo.</li>
      <li><strong>And about twenty-five people who were standing on the acts had never been entered
        at all</strong> &mdash; among them Sancho&rsquo;s parents-in-law, two grandparents drawn at the
        apex of a priest&rsquo;s kinship diagram, a child of Justa Pastora, and Adolfo Lerena.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="cap"><strong>A zero here is a statement about today, not a property of the archive.</strong>
      Every new row that cites a transcript and names no image puts the count back up, which is the
      point of printing it: <code>python3 tools/transcript-only.py</code>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-index-dates">A child born six years after his father died</h2>
  <p class="dek">The date on an index entry can be the date of the <em>copy</em>, not of the act.
    This one moved a man&rsquo;s birth by forty-four years.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>On <strong>21 September 2026</strong> a search for the children of
      <strong>Avelino Lerena and Ambrosia Traibel</strong> returned ten records. Three of them were
      dated <strong>1896 and 1919</strong>. Avelino died in 1890. Ambrosia was born around 1820.</p>
    <p>The tempting reading is that the index has attached the wrong parents, and that those three are
      other people&rsquo;s children. <strong>The opposite was true.</strong> The parents are right and
      <strong>the dates are wrong</strong>, because the film is not a baptism register at all &mdash;
      it is a register of <strong>certified copies issued in 1896</strong>, and the indexer took the
      year off the certificate and the day and month off the act inside it.</p>
    <p>The act, read off <strong>film 008166435, image 02850</strong>, opens:
      <em>&ldquo;Certifico: que en el libro 1.&ordm; de Bautismos &hellip; en el folio 135, se lee la
      partida siguiente &hellip; El veinte y uno de Noviembre de <strong>1852</strong>&rdquo;</em>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="width:15rem">The act</th><th style="width:13rem">What the index says</th>
        <th style="width:17rem">What the act says</th><th>Who it is</th>
        <th class="m" style="width:8rem">Born</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {certs.map((r) => {
          const c = Object.values(r);
          return (
            <tr>
              <th set:html={emph(c[0])} />
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[1])} />
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[2])} />
              <td class="cap" set:html={emph(c[3])} />
              <td class="m" set:html={emph(c[4])} />
            </tr>
          );
        })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>The tell is cheap and it is worth memorising.</strong>
    Two different children of one couple were indexed with the <em>same</em> birth date, 16 March 1896.
    The second tell is an impossible godparent: the man who stood at this font is
    <strong>Pedro Antonio Lerena, born 1793</strong> &mdash; the child&rsquo;s uncle, and a man who
    could not be in a room in 1896.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Why the book exists is written on every page of it.</strong> Each certificate opens
      <em>&ldquo;yo Luis Cincinato Bollo, Director General del Registro del Estado Civil, he certificado,
      de acuerdo con lo dispuesto por la <strong>Ley de 9 de Julio de 1888</strong>&rdquo;</em>. Uruguay
      took registration off the parishes in 1879 and then had the older baptisms copied across. So a
      family&rsquo;s whole set of acts can sit in one 1896 volume, decades out of their own order
      &mdash; and the index will date every one of them to 1896. <strong>This is not a fault in one
      record. It is the shape of a collection.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>And the index is not noise.</strong> It keeps the day and the month and replaces only the
      year, so it is two thirds of a date and the film supplies the third. The two brothers really were
      born on 16 March &mdash; <strong>in 1852 and 1857, five years to the day apart</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--terra);margin:24px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">The one left untested was tested, and it is the same act twice</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>This section first went up naming a third record &mdash; a christening indexed
        <strong>9 May 1919, born 13 August 1918</strong> &mdash; as <em>untested</em>, because its
        godfather bore a name that was alive in this family in 1919.</p>
      <p>The act was then read. It is dated <strong>31 December 1865</strong>, the child is
        <strong>María Adelina Lerena</strong>, born <strong>13 August 1865</strong>, and her godparents
        are <em>&ldquo;Dn. Ambrosio Lerena y Da. Carlota Muñoz&rdquo;</em> &mdash; the same two names
        the 1919 entry carries, and the same 13 August. <strong>It is one baptism, certified a second
        time on a different film, and dated by the index to that second certificate.</strong> One act,
        two films, two index entries, and fifty-three years between the two children they appear to
        describe.</p>
      <p><strong>And it is worth stating as a rule, because the two entries looked like
        corroboration.</strong> Two records, two collections, two films, two indexers, agreeing on a
        13 August and on a set of godparents. Any reasonable reader would have called that
        independent confirmation. <strong>It is one act.</strong> Documents that descend from a single
        original are <em>one witness</em>, however many times they are copied, filed and indexed
        &mdash; and copying multiplies the entries while adding nothing to the evidence. Real
        confirmation needs a different occasion or a different mouth: a birth act against a marriage
        act, a parish register against a civil registration, a census against a passport.</p>
      <p class="cap">This archive had already met the same shape from the other end and not named it.
        The three baptisms at <strong>La Mauricia in January 1846</strong> all give the year in the
        identical words, <em>&ldquo;del pasado a&ntilde;o cuarenta y tres&rdquo;</em> &mdash; including
        one for a different family. Three acts agreeing, written by one priest in one sitting, and the
        agreement means nothing: the children cannot all have been born that year. <strong>One hand,
        one occasion, one witness.</strong></p>
      <p class="cap">It also settled a contradiction this archive had flagged and could not resolve.
        Her 1946 death registration gives her birth as 1861; her brother Juan Arturo was born in
        February 1861, so no sister could be. <strong>The act says 1865, and the reasoning that
        doubted the registration was right for the right reason.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Before you list what to buy, list what you have</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>On <strong>16 September 2026</strong> this archive drew up a ranked list of files for a
      researcher going to the National Archives in Pretoria, where the first file costs
      <strong>R2,850</strong>. It put two Transvaal death notices first and second.</p>
    <p><strong>Both had been sitting on David&rsquo;s own disk, photographed, since November
      2021.</strong> The same folder held the 1911 marriage register, which was on the work list as a
      thing to <em>go and find</em>. Nobody had ever opened it.</p>
    <p>The failure was not carelessness about sources. It was a gap in the bookkeeping. This archive
      kept a careful <a href={u("/searched/")}>register of what it had read</a> and no register at all
      of <strong>what it already held</strong> &mdash; and the two are not the same thing. A document
      can be in the house, scanned, for five years, and be invisible to an archive that only records
      its own searching.</p>
    <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:20px 0">
      <p style="margin:0"><strong>So: before any list of documents to acquire, list the documents
        already held.</strong> The family&rsquo;s own papers are a repository like any other, and they
        are the only one that charges nothing and is open on a Sunday.</p>
    </div>
    <p>What came out of that folder in twenty minutes is on
      <a href={u("/family-papers/")}>The papers in the house</a>: a marriage act, a religion, a trade
      nine years earlier than anything else held, a birthplace that closed the very question the trip
      was to answer, and a blank line on a consular form that says more about the Rosario search than
      three weeks of reading registers did.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Search for the people standing next to the person</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This family is written <em>Lerena, Llerena, Lorena, Serena, Lerina, Sancio, Gerena</em> and
      <em>Levena</em> across the registers that hold it. <strong>A surname box cannot reach it
      reliably.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>The parents can.</strong> Searching for a couple &mdash; father <em>Sancho</em>, mother
      <em>Mar&iacute;a Fern&aacute;ndez</em> &mdash; rather than for their surname returned four of
      Sancho&rsquo;s children in one query, including two the archive had never met. Godparents, spouses
      and witnesses work the same way: <strong>their names are spelled ordinarily, and they are standing
      in the same act.</strong></p>
    <p class="cap">Turned on the central question the same hour, the method found nothing &mdash; which
      is itself the strongest form the central null has yet taken.
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>The open questions &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
<h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Corrections are the most valuable thing anyone can send. A family member with a date, a photograph of a
    document, or a flat contradiction of something written here will change the archive, and the change will
    be recorded rather than quietly made.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Two kinds of mark, and why both exist</h2>
  <p class="dek">A reader meeting a coloured chip should know which question it answers. There are two,
    and they are not the same question.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="lab">How well is it known?</div>
      <p style="margin-top:10px">
        <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span>
        <span class="chip lore">Family lore</span></p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Used on the narrative pages, against a <em>claim</em>. This is
        the vocabulary the Defranceski and Falco archives use too.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="lab">Is this person one of ours?</div>
      <p style="margin-top:10px">
        <span class="chip doc">Connected</span> <span class="chip">Excluded</span>
        <span class="chip inf">Unplaced</span></p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Used in the register and on person pages, against a
        <em>person</em>. Most of the register is unplaced, and that is the point of the page.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">A person can be <strong>documented</strong> and
    <strong>unplaced</strong> at once &mdash; well recorded, and not ours. Keeping the two marks separate
    is what stops a good record turning into a relative.</div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Living people</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Living people appear by name only &mdash; no dates, no places, no photographs.</strong>
      That is the rule shared across this family&rsquo;s archives, and it is stricter in practice than it
      sounds: a birth date and a city together identify a person completely.</p>
    <p>Where a living person&rsquo;s evidence bears on a dead one &mdash; a DNA test, a death notice they
      signed &mdash; <strong>the inference is published and the person is not</strong>.
      <a href={u("/dna/")}>The DNA page &rarr;</a> is the worked example.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>This rule was broken until 10 September 2026</strong>, when the direct-line page
      was found to be publishing a living person&rsquo;s exact birth date, two cities and both marriages
      beneath a caption claiming the details were withheld. Recorded on
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Corrections</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-dgs-audit">Every film number, tested</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This archive cites <strong>58 FamilySearch film numbers</strong>. Two Montevideo attributions
    had already failed inspection, which put every catalogue-derived number under suspicion. All 58
    were tested on 20 September 2026. <strong>Fifty-seven resolve to a real film and not one points at
    a film that does not exist.</strong></p>
    <p>It was done with <strong>FamilySearch signed out</strong>, because the thumbnail endpoint is
    public. Two traps are recorded below, and both would have produced false corrections against
    perfectly good data &mdash; one of them caught me.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
